<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · c. 1000 BCE · Ancient Near East</p><h1 class="sg-title">Song of Hannah</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="hbo" dir="rtl">שירת חנה</bdi> / <bdi lang="hbo-Latn">Shirat Hannah</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A song of thanks in the Bible, sung by Hannah after the birth of her son Samuel, about God lifting up the poor and the weak.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>anonymous; ascribed in the narrative to Hannah</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>ancient Israelites; Jewish and Christian worshippers</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Biblical Hebrew</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>not known; set in the narrative before Israel had a king</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words anonymous (1 Samuel 2:1–10, Hebrew Bible); ascribed in the narrative to Hannah)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-of-hannah">Song of Hannah</a> is a poem set within the prose story of the Books of Samuel. According to the surrounding narrative, the poem (1 Samuel 2:1–10) was a prayer that Hannah spoke to give thanks to God for the birth of her son, Samuel. Hannah praises <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yhwh">Yahweh</a>, reflects on the reversals he brings about and looks forward to his king. The song moves from the particular to the general, opening with her own gratitude for a reversal in her own life and closing with God's defeat of his enemies, a cosmic reversal; through this theme of reversal it serves as an introduction to the whole book. The commentators Keil and Delitzsch saw Hannah's experience as a pledge of how God would also lift up his whole nation, then bowed down and oppressed by its foes, and Walter Brueggemann sees in it the major theme of the Books of Samuel, the power and willingness of Yahweh to intrude, intervene and invert.</p><p>The mention of a king in its last verse has provoked much discussion. The biblical commentator A. F. Kirkpatrick argued that it does not imply a late date for the song, since the idea of a king was not new to the Israelite mind, and amid the anarchy and the attacks of the time the desire for a king was probably taking shape among the people. The blessing of the king and &quot;the anointed&quot; closely parallels 2 Samuel 22, which ends with God as a tower of salvation to his chosen king. Some contributors to the classical rabbinic literature read the first half of the poem as a prophecy: of Samuel's later role as a prophet, of a great-grandson of Hannah who would be a musician in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> Temple, of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sennacherib">Sennacherib</a>'s destruction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-israel">Kingdom of Israel</a>, of the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar</a> and of the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonian-captivity">Babylonian Captivity</a>. Its attribution to Hannah sets her apart among biblical figures; her hymn of praise takes up many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theme-system">themes</a> of Israel's national culture, and treats fertility and childbirth as equal in importance to the others and worthy of Israel's singers.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-of-hannah">Song of Hannah</a> is regarded as the model of reverent, personal prayer, and it is read in the synagogue as the haftarah on the first day of Rosh Hashanah, the Jewish New Year. It has much in common with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnificat">Magnificat</a>, the song of Mary in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospel-of-luke">Gospel of Luke</a>, which was sung in early Christian circles and is still sung or recited in many churches, in its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theme-system">themes</a> and in the order in which they appear; a number of Christian scholars believe that Luke used the Song of Hannah as the basis of the Magnificat, and some see Hannah as a &quot;type&quot; of Mary, both of them handmaids of God who bore sons dedicated to him. In the Catholic Church it is known as the Canticle of Anna, one of the seven Old Testament canticles of the breviary, used at Lauds on Wednesdays, and in the Revised Common Lectionary of most mainline Protestant churches it is sung or recited as the congregation's response to one of the year's readings.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A song of thanksgiving in which a woman whose prayer for a child has been answered rejoices in God and then widens her praise to the whole world: God breaks the proud and strengthens the weak, feeds the hungry, gives children to the childless, raises the poor from the dust to sit with princes, and will judge the earth and give strength to his king. The mood is exultant and confident.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks sg-blocks-2"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Biblical Hebrew</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">וַתִּתְפַּלֵּ֤ל חַנָּה֙ וַתֹּאמַ֔ר עָלַ֤ץ לִבִּי֙ בַּֽיהֹוָ֔ה רָ֥מָה קַרְנִ֖י בַּֽיהֹוָ֑ה רָ֤חַב פִּי֙ עַל־א֣וֹיְבַ֔י כִּ֥י שָׂמַ֖חְתִּי בִּישׁוּעָתֶֽךָ׃</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">אֵין־קָד֥וֹשׁ כַּיהֹוָ֖ה כִּ֣י אֵ֣ין בִּלְתֶּ֑ךָ וְאֵ֥ין צ֖וּר כֵּאלֹהֵֽינוּ׃</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">אַל־תַּרְבּ֤וּ תְדַבְּרוּ֙ גְּבֹהָ֣ה גְבֹהָ֔ה יֵצֵ֥א עָתָ֖ק מִפִּיכֶ֑ם כִּ֣י אֵ֤ל דֵּעוֹת֙ יְהֹוָ֔ה ולא וְל֥וֹ נִתְכְּנ֖וּ עֲלִלֽוֹת׃</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">קֶ֥שֶׁת גִּבֹּרִ֖ים חַתִּ֑ים וְנִכְשָׁלִ֖ים אָ֥זְרוּ חָֽיִל׃</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">שְׂבֵעִ֤ים בַּלֶּ֙חֶם֙ נִשְׂכָּ֔רוּ וּרְעֵבִ֖ים חָדֵ֑לּוּ עַד־עֲקָרָה֙ יָלְדָ֣ה שִׁבְעָ֔ה וְרַבַּ֥ת בָּנִ֖ים אֻמְלָֽלָה׃</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">יְהֹוָ֖ה מֵמִ֣ית וּמְחַיֶּ֑ה מוֹרִ֥יד שְׁא֖וֹל וַיָּֽעַל׃</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">יְהֹוָ֖ה מוֹרִ֣ישׁ וּמַעֲשִׁ֑יר מַשְׁפִּ֖יל אַף־מְרוֹמֵֽם׃</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">מֵקִ֨ים מֵעָפָ֜ר דָּ֗ל מֵֽאַשְׁפֹּת֙ יָרִ֣ים אֶבְי֔וֹן לְהוֹשִׁיב֙ עִם־נְדִיבִ֔ים וְכִסֵּ֥א כָב֖וֹד יַנְחִלֵ֑ם כִּ֤י לַֽיהֹוָה֙ מְצֻ֣קֵי אֶ֔רֶץ וַיָּ֥שֶׁת עֲלֵיהֶ֖ם תֵּבֵֽל׃</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">רַגְלֵ֤י חֲסִידָו֙ יִשְׁמֹ֔ר וּרְשָׁעִ֖ים בַּחֹ֣שֶׁךְ יִדָּ֑מּוּ כִּי־לֹ֥א בְכֹ֖חַ יִגְבַּר־אִֽישׁ׃</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">יְהֹוָ֞ה יֵחַ֣תּוּ מְרִיבָ֗ו עָלָו֙ בַּשָּׁמַ֣יִם יַרְעֵ֔ם יְהֹוָ֖ה יָדִ֣ין אַפְסֵי־אָ֑רֶץ וְיִתֶּן־עֹ֣ז לְמַלְכּ֔וֹ וְיָרֵ֖ם קֶ֥רֶן מְשִׁיחֽוֹ׃</span></p></div></div><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>And Hannah prayed, and said, My heart rejoiceth in the LORD, mine horn is exalted in the LORD: my mouth is enlarged over mine enemies; because I rejoice in thy salvation.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>There is none holy as the LORD: for there is none beside thee: neither is there any rock like our God.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Talk no more so exceeding proudly; let not arrogancy come out of your mouth: for the LORD is a God of knowledge, and by him actions are weighed.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>The bows of the mighty men are broken, and they that stumbled are girded with strength.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>They that were full have hired out themselves for bread; and they that were hungry ceased: so that the barren hath born seven; and she that hath many children is waxed feeble.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>The LORD killeth, and maketh alive: he bringeth down to the grave, and bringeth up.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>The LORD maketh poor, and maketh rich: he bringeth low, and lifteth up.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>He raiseth up the poor out of the dust, and lifteth up the beggar from the dunghill, to set them among princes, and to make them inherit the throne of glory: for the pillars of the earth are the LORD's, and he hath set the world upon them.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>He will keep the feet of his saints, and the wicked shall be silent in darkness; for by strength shall no man prevail.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>The adversaries of the LORD shall be broken to pieces; out of heaven shall he thunder upon them: the LORD shall judge the ends of the earth; and he shall give strength unto his king, and exalt the horn of his anointed.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: 1 Samuel 2:1-10, Hebrew Bible (Masoretic text). Text from <a href="https://he.wikisource.org/wiki/%D7%A9%D7%9E%D7%95%D7%90%D7%9C_%D7%90_%D7%91" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>English: King James Version (1611), 1 Samuel 2:1-10. Text from <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Bible_(King_James)%2F1_Samuel%2FChapter_2" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
